  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three identical charged balls each of charge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>C</mi></math> are suspended from a common point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> by silk threads of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> each (as shown in figure). They form an equilateral triangle of side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p><p>The ratio of net force on a charged ball to the force between any two charged balls will be</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0120_3cc925761496.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 212px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>:</mo><mn>2</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>:</mo><mn>4</mn></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0120_720695b7c3d7.png\" style=\"width: 170px; height: 154px;\" /></p><p>We have force between any two charge<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></math></p><p>Resultant electrostatics force&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt></math></p><p>So, the ratio of net force on a charged ball to the force between any charged balls&nbsp;is&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math></p>"
  }
